;; Buffer exports (queue 249; Objects/memoryobject.c's managed buffer, Modules/arraymodule.c's ob_exports and
;; Objects/bytearrayobject.c's): an exporter counts the buffers it exports and refuses a resize while one is held
;; (BufferError). A memoryview of a counting exporter holds one export through its managed buffer, shared by the
;; views made from it (a slice, a cast, memoryview(view), toreadonly), from its creation until the last of those views
;; is released: by release() or the context manager's exit, or at its death (memory_dealloc's release; pyi's
;; finalizer, TrackFinalizer, queue 241).
;;
;; pyi has no reference counts, so a view dropped without release() still counts until Go's collector finds it dead:
;; a resize that finds the count above zero first asks the collector (exportsVerify) and refuses only when a view
;; still holds the export. Those collections have the moment at del's kind of budget (exportsSpent; finSpent, queue
;; 222: a tenth of the elapsed wall time, and 200 ms); past it the resize is allowed, as before 249 (a view of the old size reads what the
;; exporter now holds, never past it: memoryview.go reads the exporter's bytes at each access).
;;
;; The mechanism is any exporter's (array; bytearray, by default since queue 595, PYI_BYTEARRAY_EXPORTS=0 not, bytearray.go
;; says why it was not before; queue 244's ctypes objects, another package's): embed a BufferExports, answer Exports() (nil: no count),
;; call Refuse before a resize; memoryview() of the object then counts.
;;
;; Under goi (the runtime interpreted) a view a returned call left in goi's registers is not found dead (queue 218's
;; REQUEST, 222's finding): a resize after such a temporary view can then be refused where CPython allows it.









